# {{projectName}} - NestJS Development Guidelines

This project is a NestJS application using {{#if isGraphQL}}GraphQL{{/if}}{{#if isRest}}REST API{{/if}} with {{#if isMongoose}}MongoDB (Mongoose){{/if}}{{#if isPrisma}}{{#if_eq database 'postgresql'}}PostgreSQL{{/if_eq}}{{#if_eq database 'mysql'}}MySQL{{/if_eq}} (Prisma){{/if}}.

## Module Structure

Each feature module should follow this structure:

```
src/
└── entity/
    {{#if isGraphQL}}├── inputs/
    │   └── entity-*.input.ts
    ├── objects/
    │   └── entity.object.ts{{/if}}
    {{#if isRest}}├── dto/
    │   └── entity-*.dto.ts{{/if}}
    ├── errors/
    │   ├── entity.errors.ts
    │   └── entity-*.error.ts
    ├── entity.module.ts
    {{#if isGraphQL}}├── entity.resolver.ts{{/if}}
    {{#if isRest}}├── entity.controller.ts{{/if}}
    ├── entity.service.ts
    {{#if isMongoose}}├── entity.repository.ts
    └── entity.schema.ts{{/if}}
    {{#if isPrisma}}└── (using Prisma - no repository/schema files needed){{/if}}
```

## File Naming Conventions

- Use module name as prefix: `user.service.ts`, `user.module.ts`
- {{#if isGraphQL}}GraphQL files: `*.object.ts` (output), `*.input.ts` (input){{/if}}
- {{#if isRest}}REST files: `*.dto.ts` (data transfer objects), `*.controller.ts`{{/if}}
- Error files: `entity-specific.error.ts` for individual errors

## Dependency Flow

{{#if isGraphQL}}Resolvers{{/if}}{{#if isRest}}Controllers{{/if}} → Services{{#if isMongoose}} → Repositories → MongoDB Models{{/if}}{{#if isPrisma}} → PrismaService{{/if}}

{{#if isGraphQL}}
## GraphQL Guidelines

### Resolvers
- Queries: `user` (single), `users` (multiple)
- Mutations: `userCreate`, `userUpdate`, `userDelete`
- Use constants from `src/common/common.constraints.ts`:
  ```typescript
  @Args(INPUT_KEY) input: UserInput,
  @Args(INPUT_SORT) sort: UserSortInput,
  @Args(INPUT_PAGINATION) pagination: PaginationInput
  ```

### Input Types
- Use `@InputType()` decorator
{{#if isMongoose}}- Use `GraphQLObjectID` for MongoDB IDs{{/if}}
- Create specific inputs: `UserCreateInput`, `UserUpdateInput`
- Put multiple inputs in `inputs/` directory

### Object Types
- Use `@ObjectType()` decorator
{{#if isMongoose}}- Standard constructor for MongoDB documents:
  ```typescript
  constructor(input: EntityDocument | EntityObject) {
    Object.assign(this, {
      ...((input as any)._doc
        ? (input as EntityDocument).toObject({ virtuals: true })
        : input),
    });
  }
  ```{{/if}}
{{/if}}

{{#if isRest}}
## REST API Guidelines

### Controllers
- Use HTTP decorators: `@Get()`, `@Post()`, `@Put()`, `@Delete()`
- Apply Swagger decorators: `@ApiOperation()`, `@ApiResponse()`
- Validate with DTOs using class-validator
- Follow RESTful patterns: `/users`, `/users/:id`

### DTOs
- Use class-validator: `@IsString()`, `@MinLength()`
- Swagger documentation: `@ApiProperty()`
- Separate DTOs: `CreateUserDto`, `UpdateUserDto`, `UserResponseDto`
{{/if}}

## Error Handling

- Extend base errors from `src/common/errors/business.error.ts`
- Available base classes: `BusinessError`, `ValidationError`, `AuthenticationError`, `NotFoundError`, `ForbiddenError`, `InternalError`
- Never use base error classes directly
{{#if isGraphQL}}- Register error codes in `src/utils/registerEnums.ts`{{/if}}

Standard error pattern:
```typescript
export class EntityNotFoundError extends NotFoundError {
  constructor() {
    super('Entity not found', ENTITY_NOT_FOUND_CODE);
  }
}
```

{{#if isMongoose}}
## MongoDB with Mongoose

### Schemas
- Use `@Schema()` decorator
- Export types: `export type EntityDocument = Entity & Document;`
- Register in `src/common/modules/app-mongoose-models.module.ts`

### Repositories
- Inject models: `@InjectModel(Entity.name) private readonly entityModel: Model<EntityDocument>`
- Handle database operations only, no business logic
- Return null for not found, let services handle business errors
{{/if}}

{{#if isPrisma}}
## {{#if_eq database 'postgresql'}}PostgreSQL{{/if_eq}}{{#if_eq database 'mysql'}}MySQL{{/if_eq}} with Prisma

- Inject PrismaService in services: `constructor(private readonly prisma: PrismaService)`
- Use Prisma client methods: `findMany`, `findUnique`, `create`, `update`, `delete`
- Handle Prisma errors (P2002, P2025, etc.)
- Use transactions for complex operations
- Run `npm run prisma:generate` after schema changes
{{/if}}

## Service Guidelines

- Handle business logic only
{{#if isMongoose}}- Call repositories for data access{{/if}}
{{#if isPrisma}}- Use PrismaService for data access{{/if}}
- Stateless and focused operations
- Validate inputs and throw appropriate errors
- Return data in expected format for {{#if isGraphQL}}GraphQL resolvers{{/if}}{{#if isRest}}REST controllers{{/if}}

## Code Quality

- Use TypeScript strict mode
- Follow ESLint and Prettier configurations
- Write meaningful names for variables and functions
- Keep functions small and focused
- Add JSDoc for complex functions

{{#if hasRedis}}
## Redis Caching

- Use RedisService for all cache operations
- Handle cache failures gracefully
- Set appropriate TTL values
- Implement cache-aside pattern
{{/if}}

{{#if hasElasticsearch}}
## Elasticsearch

- Use ElasticsearchService for search operations
- Index data properly with correct mappings
- Handle search errors gracefully
- Implement proper aggregations for analytics
{{/if}}

{{#if hasRabbitmq}}
## RabbitMQ Messaging

- Use RabbitMQService for message operations
- Implement proper error handling and retries
- Use appropriate exchange types
- Handle message acknowledgments properly
{{/if}}
